    "final_text": "I fixed ISSUE-214: query values of `0` and `false` now show up in the URL. The full suite passes (9 of 9, including the 2 new tests).\n\n**Cause.** `serializeQuery` in `src/url-builder.js` skipped any value that JavaScript treats as false (`if (!value) continue;`). The check was meant to drop missing values, but it also dropped `0`, `false` and `''`. `appendQueryValue` had the same check for array items, so `attempt: [0, 1, false]` came out as just `attempt=1`. I reproduced both before changing anything.\n\n**Fix.** Two lines in `src/url-builder.js` (lines 126 and 137): both checks now skip only `undefined` and `null`. That's the same rule `normalizePath` already uses for path segments. The `buildUrl` API is unchanged.\n\n**Regression tests** (`tests/url-builder.test.js`):\n- `buildUrl keeps retry=0 and include_archived=false query values (ISSUE-214)`: the issue's repro, checked against the exact URL `...?dry_run=true&include_archived=false&retry=0`.\n- `serializeQuery keeps 0 and false array items but still skips null and undefined`: covers the array case, and checks that `null` and `undefined` are still left out, so the fix doesn't start sending `key=null`.\n\nBoth tests failed on the old code with the reported symptom and pass with the fix. I ran the suite once, through the Dex run-gate script (`full-gate`).\n\n**`ISSUE.md`** now has a `Resolution` section naming the cause, the fix and both tests.\n\n**Decisions I made:**\n- **Empty strings:** `{ q: '' }` now gives `q=` instead of being dropped. That's how `URLSearchParams` handles it and matches the issue's \"include those values exactly\". Callers who want a key left out should pass `undefined` or `null`. This is noted in the resolution.\n- **`NaN`:** it now comes out as `n=NaN`. I didn't add special handling, since passing `NaN` is almost certainly a bug in the calling code.\n- **Not committed:** the changes are uncommitted on `main`, because the task didn't ask for commits and the repo has no remote.\n- **No lint:** the project has no linter set up, so none was run.\n\n**Possible follow-up (not done):** `normalizeOptions` uses `options.path || []`, so a path of `0` is also treated as missing. It's the same kind of bug, but it wasn't part of this issue.",
